{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses went.</b> One needs to know where he went. And Yonatan translated that he went to the dwelling of the house of instruction, and the commentators said (see Ramban and Rabbenu Bachya) that he went from the camp of Levi to the camp of Israel like a person taking leave of his companion. And the verse is ambiguous, and there is in it no indication for either of the approaches. Further, one needs to know who aroused the spirit of Moses to know that his days and years were fulfilled — for did they not say in the Gemara (Shabbat 30) that one does not inform a person of his days and years? So who informed Moses?" "<b>Now it may be seen</b> to interpret according to their words, of blessed memory (Zohar ha-Kadosh, vol. I, 218), that they said: forty days before a person's passing, his soul departs from him, as it says (Song of Songs 4:6): ״וְנָסוּ הַצְּלָלִים,״ and it visits the place of its encampment on high, and the righteous recognize this. Go and learn from Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, who recognized it in Rabbi Yitzchak, as stated in the Book of the Zohar (there)." "<b>And they said</b> further that the names that Israel possess are the names of the souls. And I have already noted this in my commentary on the verse ״וְשֵׁם רְשָׁעִים יִרְקָב״ (Proverbs 10:7), and as our Sages of blessed memory said (Zohar Chadash, Ruth 84c) that the wicked do not remember their name. And the reason is that the wicked person has no soul, as hinted in the verse ״וְשַׂמְתָּ סַכִּין בְּלֹעֶךָ אִם בַּעַל נֶפֶשׁ אָתָּה״ (Proverbs 23:2) — thus you learn that the wicked person is not a ba'al nefesh (possessor of a soul), for it is held captive away from him. Therefore the wicked do not recognize their name, for they are not possessors of a soul that possesses the Name." "<b>And here</b> it says ״וַיֵּלֶךְ מֹשֶׁה,״ meaning: the spirit of life within him, which is called Moses, departed in the usual manner for one whose end has arrived. And it referred to his soul in the masculine, for that is a sign of the greatness of the soul when it is in the masculine degree. And the verse informed us, by saying ״וַיְדַבֵּר אֶת״ etc., that he felt its departure and recognized that his end had arrived on that day, as it says ״בֶּן מֵאָה״ etc. ״אָנֹכִי הַיּוֹם,״ and they said of blessed memory (Sotah 13b): הַיּוֹם — my days and years are fulfilled." "<b>And if</b> you say: From where did Moses know precisely the day of death? For they said of blessed memory that from the day the shadows flee until the day of death is forty days — and who knows these things more than Moses? He discerned and knew the day of his end, and such recognition is attainable to the great ones of the world. And see what I have written on the verse ״וַיִּקְרְבוּ יְמֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Genesis 47:29)." "<b>And he spoke et (all) the words, etc.</b> Its saying ״et (all) the words״ is to awaken [us] that even though the day of his passing had arrived, as it says ״I am this day,״ meaning today my days are full, etc., nevertheless he had the strength to speak all the words stated from here to the end of the book — something in which none other than he had the power to do, whether in physical strength or in intellectual power."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:46.551732796Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}